      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.069,
            0.144,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": "70"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.069,
            0.489,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.885,
            0.148
          ],
          "content": "square is $b ^ { 2 } .$ . The areas of the two rectangles are ab each. Together they make the bigger square; hence we can conclude that"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.396,
            0.155,
            0.638,
            0.174
          ],
          "content": "( a + b ) ^ { 2 } = a ^ { 2 } + 2 a b + b ^ { 2 } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.183,
            0.885,
            0.222
          ],
          "content": "From Fig. 4.2 it is clear that $(a + b)^{2} = a^{2} + 2ab + b^{2}$ for all a and b when a and b are lengths of line segments."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.222,
            0.886,
            0.281
          ],
          "content": "Think of numbers a and b where a and b do not represent lengths of line segments. What if a and b are negative numbers? Let us check for some negative numbers and see if this equation still works."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.285,
            0.512,
            0.305
          ],
          "content": "**Example 2:** Let $a = - 2$ and $b = - 3$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.31,
            0.514,
            0.33
          ],
          "content": "Then $(a + b) = -5   and   (a + b)^2 = 25.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.334,
            0.484,
            0.354
          ],
          "content": "Also $a^{2}=4,b^{2}=9\\ and2ab=12.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.36,
            0.525,
            0.38
          ],
          "content": "Thus $a^{2}+2ab+b^{2}=4+12+9=25.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.385,
            0.536,
            0.406
          ],
          "content": "Hence, $a^{2}+2ab+b^{2}=(a+b)^{2}$ again!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.409,
            0.861,
            0.451
          ],
          "content": "Now suppose a and b are rational numbers, say $a \\div \\frac { 2 } { 3 }$ and $b = { \\frac { 3 } { 4 } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.447,
            0.469,
            0.493
          ],
          "content": "Then $(a + b) = \\left( - \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{3}{4} \\right) = \\frac{1}{12}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.504,
            0.365,
            0.545
          ],
          "content": "( a + b ) ^ { 2 } = { \\frac { 1 } { 1 4 4 } }   .",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.553,
            0.769,
            0.66
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}a^{2}+2ab+b^{2}=\\left(\\frac{-2}{3}\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)^{2}\\\\=\\frac{4}{9}-1+\\frac{9}{16}=\\frac{64-144+81}{144}=\\frac{145-144}{144}=\\frac{1}{144}\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.672,
            0.885,
            0.732
          ],
          "content": "So, $a^{2}+2ab+b^{2}=(a+b)^{2}$ seems to be true for rational numbers too. But we are still not sure if it is true for all numbers. To verify this, let us investigate further using the distributive property of numbers:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.739,
            0.618,
            0.782
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned} &(a+b)^{2}=(a+b)(a+b)=a(a+b)+b(a+b)\\\\ &=a^{2}+ab+ba+b^{2}=a^{2}+2ab+b^{2}.\\\\ \\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.787,
            0.885,
            0.827
          ],
          "content": "Recall that in Grade 8 you were introduced to $(a+b)^{2}=a^{2}+2ab+b^{2}$ as an identity."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.832,
            0.81,
            0.851
          ],
          "content": "What is the difference between an equation and an identity?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.851,
            0.887,
            0.91
          ],
          "content": "An **algebraic identity** is an equation that is true for all values of the variables occurring in it, while an equation need not be true for all values."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
